<div class="meta">Drill 0025 · pairs with lesson 0025</div>
<h1>Drill: Inverted-Hull Outlines</h1>

<div class="q"><h3>1. Why does the rim appear at the silhouette?</h3>
<p>The outline copy is drawn with front faces culled. Walk through why the
camera ends up seeing a rim only around the object's edge.</p>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p>With front faces culled, only the far (inside-out) side of the inflated
shell renders. The real object, drawn after, covers that shell everywhere
their screen projections overlap — which is everywhere except the band where
the 6%-larger shell extends past the real silhouette. That uncovered band is
the outline, and it tracks the silhouette from any viewpoint.</p>
</div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>2. Inflate about what point — and why not the feet?</h3>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p>About each PART's own center (translate to center, scale, translate
back). Scaling about the figure's feet would displace high parts (hair,
head) outward more than low parts — the rim would grow thicker up the body
and hair could visibly detach from the head.</p>
</div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>3. The missing flush</h3>
<p>Someone removes the <code>rlDrawRenderBatchActive()</code> calls around
<code>rlSetCullFace</code>. Nothing crashes. What actually goes wrong?</p>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p>raylib batches immediate geometry; GL state applies when the batch
FLUSHES, not when the call is made. Without draining first, the cull-mode
switch can apply to earlier queued geometry (or not yet apply to the outline
pass) — outlines silently render with the wrong culling, or unrelated scene
geometry drawn in the same batch gets front-culled. Classic invisible
reordering bug.</p>
</div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>4. Why grouped passes instead of outline+color per part?</h3>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p>Each cull-face switch forces a batch drain. Interleaving would drain the
batch twice per PART (~2×5 per character); grouping the outline pass then
the color pass drains twice per CHARACTER. Same image, a fraction of the
flushes.</p>
</div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>5. One function, two passes</h3>
<p>The outline pass required almost no new drawing code. What earlier
decision made that true, and what does it buy future graphics packs?</p>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p>#101 isolated every part shape behind <code>drawPartRecipe(part, at,
dim, colors)</code>. The outline pass is the same dispatch called with an
all-black color set inside an inflation matrix — so every part, including
ones added by future packs, inherits outlines with zero extra work.</p>
</div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>6. Where the technique fails</h3>
<p>Name two kinds of geometry in this game that inverted hull handles
poorly or not at all, and what the mitigation was.</p>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p>(1) Hard-edged boxes: at sharp corners the inflated shell can gap; kept
the scale small (1.06) and authored an escalation path (bigger scale →
per-recipe outline opt-out) — not needed in practice. (2) Billboards (the
mood emote): camera-facing sprites have no hull at all — it renders
un-outlined and now clashes with the style, recorded as playtest finding #1
rather than fixed (observations-only brief).</p>
</div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>7. Transfer it</h3>
<p>You want a soft glow behind pickups rendered with the same immediate-mode
primitives. Apply the lesson's general shape.</p>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p>Same skeleton: re-issue the pickup's existing draw path scaled up,
tinted glow-color (maybe additive blend), state changes wrapped in explicit
batch flushes, passes grouped. No parallel "glow mesh" pipeline — the one
draw path serves both, so every new pickup glows by construction.</p>
</div></details></div>
